ackages/numpy/_core/_methods.py\", line 48, in _amin\n    return umr_minimum(a, axis, None, out, keepdims, initial, where)\nValueError: zero-size array to reduction operation minimum which has no identity\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root  3594 Sep 11 19:46 obs_0113.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 29813 Sep 11 19:46 obs_0114.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 36936 Sep 11 20:11 obs_0115.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 24153 Sep 11 20:16 obs_0116.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 13291 Sep 11 20:18 obs_0117.png\nShell cwd was reset to /work"}]},"f268310f7edc1780f3a27338cf3cee89a02cdb60e27ed9a6b0a6c0f5f78523ae":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The bottom edge of the dark brown mask back-projects to a constant x=3.166 as y sweeps from 3.57 to 0.88 — another vertical-face signature, but at a different x than the leg mask's 2.592. That's confusing, since it suggests this could be a different face of the table entirely, so I need to reconsider which face is producing this edge.\n\nConstant x from a z=0 back-projection doesn't necessarily mean the true point is at z=0 — it could mean the actual elevated point lies along a ray whose ground-plane intersection happens to have that x. Rays from the camera scale x, y, z together with parameter t, so I need to think through whether this constant-x pattern reflects a real vertical plane at x=3.166 or is a projection artifact of an elevated edge.\n\nChecking f116's leg mask bottom, I get x0=2.592 at z=0, which maps to about 2.148 at z=0.75 — consistent with the other frame's value near 2.13-2.15 at that same height. This suggests it's not circular reasoning but an actual triangulation: two different camera positions viewing the same vertical edge agree once I account for height.\n\nSetting up the two linear x(z) relations from each frame and solving where they intersect, I get z≈0.727 and x≈2.162 — pinning down the true location of this edge in space.\n\nBut that height seems plausible for a table surface, so I need to double-check which specific edge of the mask this corresponds to — the mask in obs_0117 has a bottom boundary that slopes down-right from around v=371 to v=446 before jumping, so I need to reconsider whether I'm tracking the true bottom edge correctly.\n\nLooking at the top boundary now — it dips to a minimum v around u=204 then rises back, forming a V shape, which would be the far edge of the tabletop if the camera is looking down at it. The bottom boundary should then be the near edge, but I'm puzzled that the mask shows no legs extending down to the floor given the table height and camera position.\n\nI check pixel colors near the bottom row and find the brown region ends around v≈430-446, suggesting that's the near edge of the tabletop rather than legs. The bottom edge of the mask looks smooth rather than showing downward spikes for legs, though my column sampling might just be skipping over them, and I recall the summary mentioned three visible legs in this frame.\n\nNow I realize the darker leg colors (48-80 range) fall outside my R>95 threshold, so my mask is only capturing the tabletop slab, not the legs — that explains the smooth bottom edge. This means the bottom boundary is the near edge of the top at table height, and back-projecting gives a constant x value there, which I need to reconcile with an earlier frame where a similarly constant-x feature was identified as the west end-face — possibly a different physical part of the table.\n\nThis matches the west end-face x≈2.592(z=0) I noted earlier, confirming a west-facing edge. So the table's west edge sits at x≈2.16 with top height z≈0.73, and with the dark_table_1 center at (2.57,1.38) that implies a width of roughly 0.8 in x. Now I need to rescale the y extent of that edge (0.88 to 3.57) to z=0.727 using the k=0.531 back-projection factor.\n\nThe rescaled edge runs from y≈0.655 to y≈2.086, giving a length of about 1.43 m and a center y≈1.37 — this lines up nicely with the mapped center of 1.38. Now I need to find the far (east) edge using the top boundary of the mask.\n\nI should back-project the top boundary numerically at height z=0.727 to get the far corner properly, and it might be worth re-checking the f117 pose for safety. I'm also noticing the white wall region spans a large area and the floor junction seems to sit around v≈400 at u390 — I need to pin down that transition more precisely. With only three frames left (f118, f119, f120), I'll compute the table geometry from obs_0117.\n\nLet me resect the pose from the wall base first, then redo the height-aware back-projection of the table top boundary using triangulated z-values, since shifting f117's pose would require recomputing the triangulation. At heading 60°, the camera is looking northeast, so I need to determine whether the white region at u390-490 corresponds to the north wall or the east wall — checking the color values, they're slightly warm near u490, suggesting a transition zone between the two walls.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Table top slab isolated. Its bottom edge shows the constant-x signature again — triangulating against f116."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014CmTTydBithC82SiNUFSou","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp && cat > w.py <<'EOF'\nimport numpy as np, math\nfrom PIL import Image\nim=np.array(Image.open('/work/observations/obs_0117.png').convert('RGB')).astype(int)\nR,G,B=im[:,:,0],im[:,:,1],im[:,:,2]\n# --- wall base junction: pure white north wall vs warm east wall\npw=(R>250)&(G>250)&(B>250)\nwm=(R>250)&(G>248)&(B>240)&(B<251)\nfor nm,m in (('pure',pw),('warm',wm)):\n    ys,xs=np.nonzero(m)\n    if len(xs): print(nm,'n',len(xs),'u',xs.min(),xs.max(),'v',ys.min(),ys.max())\nprint('--- pure-white per-col bottom')\nfor u in range(120,512,16):\n    c=np.nonzero(pw[:,u])[0]\n    if len(c)>5: print(u,c.max())\nprint('--- warm per-col bottom')\nfor u in range(360,512,16):\n    c=np.nonzero(wm[:,u])[0]\n    if len(c)>5: print(u,c.max())\nEOF\npython3 w.py 2>/dev/null"}}]},"f2483d3c8094ce2c03a69eccdc8aa3aa32e8abb5a13b74811b0cc4092f7086b2":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm tracking the position after turns f154-f156, moving south to (1.450, 5.150) with heading 180. Checking whether this matches the image showing the block, brown table, and plant in the lower-left, and verifying the potted plant's position relative to the current facing direction.\n\nI realize the block visible at lower-left appears bright gray-white rather than the dark bluish-gray I associated with the \"structure_1\" mass earlier, which means I need to re-examine whether they're actually the same object.\n\nLet me reconfirm the camera pose using the south wall junction with the tan floor first, then isolate the block by masking for neutral bright pixels where red and blue values stay close together, distinguishing it from the warmer tan floor tone.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01F7nztvazTbnw8kr8oRaPT5","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF' 2>/dev/null\nfrom PIL import Image; import numpy as np, math\nim=np.asarray(Image.open('observations/obs_0156.png').convert('RGB')).astype(int)\nR,G,B=im[:,:,0],im[:,:,1],im[:,:,2]\npx,py,h=1.450,5.150,180.0\nhr=math.radians(h); fw=(math.sin(hr),math.cos(hr)); rt=(math.cos(hr),-math.sin(hr))\ndef bp(u,v,z=0.0):\n    if v<=262: return None\n    D=(1.55-z)*256.0/(v-256.0); L=D*(u-256.0)/256.0\n    return (round(float(px+D*fw[0]+L*rt[0]),3), round(float(py+D*fw[1]+L*rt[1]),3), round(float(D),2))\ntan=(R>140)&(R<215)&(B<G)&((R-B)>15)\nprint('POSE CHECK tan topmost:')\nfor u in range(230,512,25):\n    c=np.nonzero(tan[:,u])[0]\n    if len(c)<3: print(u,'none'); continue\n    print(u,c.min(),bp(u,c.min()))\nblk=(R>175)&(R<240)&(abs(R-B)<12)&(abs(R-G)<6)\nprint('BLOCK mask, per column: top v and bottom v')\nfor u in range(0,240,10):\n    c=np.nonzero(blk[:,u])[0]\n    if len(c)<3: print(u,'none'); continue\n    # contiguous run from bottom of mask\n    print(u,'top',c.min(),'bot',c.max(),'bp_top_z0',bp(u,c.min()),'bp_bot',bp(u,c.max()))\nEOF"}}]},"f21652e6ea9ea9a0c5bb1fd2c1e31c2c297ad5ee492bbf9564fc4f9bde29b8e4":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me take a look at the image.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C5JBJD9CR4oJ4kXr5t1ozn","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/work/observations/obs_0037.png"}}]}}